  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $1$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }1}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-5,0)$ is $-1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+1$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =0-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=-1.$",
